## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of lock-free design, cross-socket contention, and memory ordering on MESI-based systems.

## Key Concepts
- Per-core local buffers to minimize cross-socket traffic and reduce coherence domain contention
- Per-slot sequence counters to prevent ABA race conditions
- Correct memory ordering: release for publishing, acquire for consuming, ensuring proper visibility across threads
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Wrap-around handling via power-of-two masking combined with sequence counter verification
- Backpressure mechanisms to handle slow consumers gracefully

## Data Structures

```c
typedef struct {
    uint64_t seq;
    char pad[64];  // align to cache line boundary
    void* data;
    char payload[112];  // 128-byte slots total
} Slot;

typedef struct {
    Slot slots[1 << 18];
    _Atomic(uint64_t) head;
    _Atomic(uint64_t) tail;
    char pad[L1_CACHE_SIZE];
} Ring;

typedef struct {
    char buffer[1 << 12][128];
    uint64_t seq[1 << 12];
    uint32_t local_head;
    char pad[L1_CACHE_SIZE];
} PerCoreQueue;
```

## Pseudocode

**Enqueue:**
```c
bool enqueue(Ring* ring, PerCoreQueue* local, void* data, size_t len) {
    uint32_t slot = local->local_head & (1 << 12 - 1);

    memcpy(local->buffer[slot], data, len);
    atomic_store_explicit(&local->seq[slot], 
                          local->seq[slot] + 2, 
                          memory_order_relaxed);

    uint64_t desired = local->local_head + 1;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
            &ring->tail, &desired, desired,
            memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed)) {
        local->local_head++;
        return true;
    }
    return false;  // backpressure
}
```

**Dequeue:**
```c
bool dequeue(Ring* ring, void* out, size_t* len) {
    uint64_t head = atomic_load_explicit(&ring->head, memory_order_relaxed);

    uint64_t desired = head + 1;
    if (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
            &ring->head, &head, desired,
            memory_order_acquire, memory_order_relaxed)) {
        return false;
    }

    Slot* slot = &ring->slots[head & (N - 1)];
    uint64_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&slot->seq, memory_order_acquire);
    if ((seq & 1) != 0) return false;  // not yet written

    memcpy(out, slot->data, *len);
    atomic_store_explicit(&slot->seq, seq + 1, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan

1. **Throughput measurement:** Vary producer/consumer thread counts (1-16), measure ops/sec at fixed load.

2. **Cache-coherence traffic:** Use perf counters (`perf stat -e cache-references,cache-misses, LLC-load-misses, LLC-store-misses`) across:
   - Single-socket baseline (no cross-socket traffic)
   - Multi-socket with threads pinned to different NUMA nodes
   - Vary write-to-read ratios (100/0, 50/50, 0/100)

3. **Contention stress test:** Saturate ring capacity, measure under overflow conditions.

4. **Baseline comparison:** Compare against mutex-protected ring and lock-free SPSC queue.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests basic concurrent data structure design and practical use of atomics and memory ordering in a beginner-friendly but realistic setting.

## Key Concepts
- MPSC synchronization using a shared tail and a local consumer head
- Atomic fetch_add for slot reservation
- Memory ordering: release on enqueue, acquire on dequeue
- Correct wrap-around handling in a fixed-size buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-C code (C11 atomics style)
#define N (1<<12)
typedef struct { uint64_t data; uint64_t seq; } slot_t;
slot_t buf[N];
atomic_uintptr_t tail = 0; // producer index
size_t head = 0; // consumer index

bool enqueue(uint64_t v){
  size_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t >= N) return false;
  buf[t].data = v;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  buf[t].seq = t; // simple wrap-safe signal
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(uint64_t* out){
  if (head >= atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_acquire)) return false;
  *out = buf[head].data;
  head++;
  return true;
}
```

